45
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,210
East London sold nine buildings.

46
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:23,760
North London sold one.

47
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:26,400
My best explanation is the follow-ups.

48
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:30,110
We'd sent the East London owners
more follow-up letters, so

49
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:32,800
when their plans changed,
they already had our name.

50
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:37,390
The lesson for anyone buying is that
more enquiries doesn't mean more deals.

51
00:02:37,440 --> 00:02:41,440
What matters is how many owners
actually go through with a sale.

52
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:44,070
The first deal didn't come
from a campaign at all.

53
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:45,400
It came from one letter.

54
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:49,080
Three years ago, there was a derelict
pub on Deancross Street in Shadwell.

55
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:51,640
I found the owner through
Companies House and wrote to them.

56
00:02:51,640 --> 00:02:54,350
They'd just had a planning
application refused, and

57
00:02:54,350 --> 00:02:56,410
that's why they were
open to a conversation.

58
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:59,300
It sold vacant for £1.25 million.

59
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:01,840
The buyer, Emran, matched
the price with no mortgage.

60
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:04,250
Since then, he's got planning
to change the use of

61
00:03:04,250 --> 00:03:07,440
